The network is healthy; no fault found in the routed core or office gateway. From multiple Office LAN hosts (192.168.3.0/24), end-to-end reachability to other departments works now: office_pc (192.168.3.2) successfully pings Server Room pc1 (1.0.0.5), Internet Lab hosts (128.168.0.x), and the Principal LAN (192.168.4.2). Additional Office hosts (enquiry, exam_cell, tpo) also reach servers and other LANs. Router1, the Office gateway, has all required routes via RIP, and its FRR service is running. Router1’s interfaces are correctly addressed (eth0=192.168.3.1/24, eth2=10.10.0.2/8, eth3=20.20.0.1/8), and its routing table contains RIP-learned paths to 192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.2.0/24, 192.168.4.0/24, 1.0.0.0/8, and 128.168.0.0/16. Router0 and Router2 are also up with FRR active and reciprocally learned routes. Office clients have a correct default route to 192.168.3.1.

Mechanism: Because the control plane (RIP on routers 0/1/2) is converged and data-plane links (10.10.0.0/8 and 20.20.0.0/8) are up, packets from Office subnets are forwarded and replied to end-to-end, reflected in low-latency, 0% loss ICMP tests.

Localization: No misbehaving device or interface identified.

Root cause: Not reproducible—likely a transient or a user-specific workstation misconfiguration earlier.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. If the issue recurs on specific PCs, verify their IP/gateway settings, flush ARP (ip neigh flush dev eth0), and retest; capture traceroute/arp -n at the time for further analysis. === END ===